WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:02.950 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  entering the race with his wife and two  daughters in tow rishi sunak opened his  campaign in margaret thatcher's hometown  of grantham  [Applause]  boris johnson's former chancellor  attempted to brand himself as the  underdog and said he would prioritize  reducing the nhs backlog criticizing his  opponents pledged to cut taxes that he  himself raised  the nhs will be safe in my hands  we face the worst nhs winter crisis in  decades  tackling the backlogs is the biggest  public service emergency  we need a fundamentally different  approach  we will take the best of our covered  response and apply those lessons to  clearing the massive backlogs in the nhs  speaking to party members in kent liz  trust steered the conversation back to  brexit we need to tackle the nhs backlog  and i would put in place a strong health  secretary that would get that done but  in order to pay for those public  services we need a growing economy  that's so vital that we unleash those  post-brexit opportunities  we get rid of those pointless eu  regulations and we also cut taxes to  make sure that we are  getting people to set up businesses and  help grow the economy  so far the battle for number 10 has been  fought largely on the economy but today  as the starting pistols were fired both  candidates are desperate to prove they  can do more than just be fiscally  responsible  mr sunak is vehemently against borrowing  his priority is to tackle inflation liz  trust on the other hand is pro-borrowing  post-pandemic because she's keen to  start cutting taxes in a dig to liz  trust rishi sunak said if the government  is to deliver on its brexit promises it  needs to be led by someone who believes  in brexit and voted for brexit both  candidates vow to review existing eu  laws retained after brexit  on health rishi sunak is planning to put  the nhs on a war footing and use lessons  learned from the kovid pandemic to clear  waiting lists liz trust says she's  committed to increased nhs funding  though there are questions over how she  would do this considering her plans to  cut taxes we're on a war footing but we  haven't got enough troops to fight the  battle and the battle is a long one this  is a long campaign that the nhs is going  to have to pursue now to bring down  waiting times  as boris johnson's final parliamentary  week as prime minister came to an end he  was out and about with ukrainian  soldiers training with the british army  [Applause]  shadowing soldiers boris johnson was  keen to show the government's unwavering  support for ukraine solidifying his  legacy as a prime minister who  unequivocally backed ukraine and stood  up to vladimir putin but that's not the  only battleground on boris johnson's  mind ballot papers for voting to replace  him go out in the next two weeks so his  potential successors need to make a good  impression on tory members very quickly  if they're going to win the top job mari  aurora sky news westminster

